\id ISA Transparent Bible (working title) - pilot draft
\h Isaiah
\toc1 The Book of the Prophet Isaiah
\toc2 Isaiah
\toc3 Isa
\mt1 Isaiah
\c 7
\s1 Isaiah Sent to Ahaz
\p
\v 1 Now in the days of Ahaz the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, king of Judah, Rezin the king of Aram and Pekah the son of Remaliah, king of Israel, went up to Jerusalem to make war against it, but could not prevail against it.\f + \fr 7:1 \ft Note: Aram is the kingdom of Damascus; KJV \fqa Syria\ft . Also in vv. 2, 4, 5, 8.\f*
\v 2 And it was told to the house of David, saying, “Aram has settled upon Ephraim.” And his heart and the heart of his people shook, as the trees of the forest shake before the wind.\f + \fr 7:2 \ft Or: \fqa Aram is allied with Ephraim\ft . Lit. \fqa Aram has rested on Ephraim\ft . Ephraim is the northern kingdom, Israel.\f*
\p
\v 3 Then the \nd LORD\nd* said to Isaiah, “Go out now to meet Ahaz, you and Shear-jashub your son, at the end of the conduit of the upper pool, on the highway to the Launderer’s Field,\f + \fr 7:3 \ft Heb.: \fqa Shear-jashub\ft  means “a remnant shall return”.\f*\f + \fr 7:3 \ft Note: KJV \fqa the fuller’s field\ft .\f*
\v 4 and say to him, ‘Take care and be calm. Do not fear, and do not let your heart be faint because of these two smoldering stumps of firebrands, at the fierce anger of Rezin and Aram and the son of Remaliah.
\v 5 Because Aram, with Ephraim and the son of Remaliah, has plotted evil against you, saying,
\v 6 “Let us go up against Judah and terrify it,\f + \fr 7:6 \ft Or: \fqa tear it apart\ft ; KJV \fqa vex it\ft .\f* and let us break it open for ourselves and set up a king in the midst of it, the son of Tabeel,”
\q1
\v 7 thus says the Lord \nd GOD\nd*:
\q2 “It shall not stand,
\q2 and it shall not come to pass.\f + \fr 7:7 \ft Heb.: \fqa Adonai YHWH\ft ; see the note at Genesis 2:4.\f*
\q1
\v 8 For the head of Aram is Damascus,
\q2 and the head of Damascus is Rezin.
\q1 And within sixty-five years
\q2 Ephraim shall be shattered from being a people.
\q1
\v 9 And the head of Ephraim is Samaria,
\q2 and the head of Samaria is the son of Remaliah.
\q1 If \zpl you\zpl* will not believe,
\q2 surely \zpl you\zpl* shall not be established.’ ”\f + \fr 7:9 \ft Heb.: a wordplay, \fqa ta’aminu\ft  … \fqa te’amenu\ft , “believe” … “be made firm”; both come from the root of “Amen”.\f*
\s1 The Sign of Immanuel
\p
\v 10 Again the \nd LORD\nd* spoke to Ahaz, saying,
\v 11 “Ask for yourself a sign from the \nd LORD\nd* your God; make it as deep as Sheol or as high as the heights above.”\f + \fr 7:11 \ft Text: the same Hebrew consonants are read with two sets of vowels. As the Masoretic vowels stand, \fqa make it deep, ask it \ft (KJV \fqa ask it either in the depth\ft ); with other vowels, \fqa to Sheol\ft , as in the later Greek versions of Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion. Sheol is the realm of the dead.\f*
\v 12 But Ahaz said, “I will not ask, and I will not put the \nd LORD\nd* to the test.”
\v 13 And he said, “\zpl Hear\zpl* now, O house of David! Is it too little for \zpl you\zpl* to weary men, that \zpl you\zpl* will weary my God also?
\v 14 Therefore the Lord himself will give \zpl you\zpl* a sign: Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Heb.: \fqa ha-‘almah\ft , “the young woman”, a woman of marriageable age; the word itself does not state virginity, though the young women it refers to elsewhere are, where their state is known, unmarried (Genesis 24:43; Exodus 2:8). The Greek (LXX) has \fqa parthenos\ft , “virgin”, and Matthew 1:23 quotes it so. The ordinary Hebrew word for a virgin is \fqa betulah\ft .\f*\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Or: \fqa is with child and shall bear\ft . The Hebrew has an adjective, “pregnant”, with no tense.\f*\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Heb.: \fqa ‘Immanu El\ft  means “God with us”.\f*
\v 15 Curds and honey he shall eat when he knows to refuse the evil and choose the good.\f + \fr 7:15 \ft Or: \fqa until he knows\ft ; or \fqa so that he may know\ft .\f*
\v 16 For before the boy knows to refuse the evil and choose the good, the land whose two kings you dread shall be forsaken.\f + \fr 7:16 \ft Or: \fqa the land before whose two kings you are in dread\ft .\f*
\v 17 The \nd LORD\nd* will bring upon you and upon your people and upon your father’s house days that have not come since the day that Ephraim departed from Judah—the king of Assyria.”\f + \fr 7:17 \ft Note: The last words, “the king of Assyria”, stand abruptly in the Hebrew; some take them as an explanatory addition.\f*
\p
\v 18 And it shall come to pass in that day that the \nd LORD\nd* will whistle for the fly that is at the farthest streams of Egypt, and for the bee that is in the land of Assyria.
\v 19 And they will all come and settle in the steep ravines and in the clefts of the rocks, and on all the thornbushes and on all the pastures.\f + \fr 7:19 \ft Or: \fqa watering places\ft . The meaning of the last word is uncertain.\f*
\v 20 In that day the Lord will shave with a razor that is hired beyond the River—with the king of Assyria—the head and the hair of the feet, and it will also take away the beard.\f + \fr 7:20 \ft Note: The River is the Euphrates. “The hair of the feet” is a euphemism for the hair of the private parts; the shaving is a sign of humiliation.\f*
\v 21 And it shall come to pass in that day that a man will keep alive a young cow and two sheep,
\v 22 and it shall come to pass, because of the abundance of milk they give, that he will eat curds; for everyone who is left in the midst of the land will eat curds and honey.
\v 23 And it shall come to pass in that day that every place where there were a thousand vines worth a thousand \add shekels\add* of silver will become briers and thorns.\f + \fr 7:23 \ft Note: KJV \fqa a thousand silverlings\ft .\f*
\v 24 With arrows and with the bow one will go there, because all the land will be briers and thorns.
\v 25 And as for all the hills that were hoed with the hoe, you will not go there for fear of briers and thorns; but they will become a place for sending out oxen and for sheep to trample.
\c 52
\s1 Awake, O Zion
\q1
\v 1 Awake, awake, put on your strength, O Zion;
\q2 put on your beautiful garments, O Jerusalem, the holy city;
\q1 for the uncircumcised and the unclean
\q2 shall no more come into you.
\q1
\v 2 Shake yourself from the dust; arise; sit down, O Jerusalem;
\q2 loose yourself from the bonds of your neck, O captive daughter of Zion.\f + \fr 52:2 \ft Or: \fqa arise and sit enthroned\ft .\f*\f + \fr 52:2 \ft Text: the Qere, \fqa loose yourself\ft , is translated; the Ketiv reads \fqa the bonds of your neck are loosed\ft .\f*
\p
\v 3 For thus says the \nd LORD\nd*: “\zpl You\zpl* were sold for nothing, and \zpl you\zpl* shall be redeemed without money.”
\v 4 For thus says the Lord \nd GOD\nd*: “My people went down at the first into Egypt to sojourn there, and Assyria oppressed them for nothing.
\v 5 Now therefore, what have I here,” declares the \nd LORD\nd*, “seeing that my people are taken away for nothing? Those who rule over them howl,” declares the \nd LORD\nd*, “and continually all day long my name is blasphemed.\f + \fr 52:5 \ft Or: \fqa their rulers wail\ft ; or, with a different vowel reading, \fqa mock\ft . Quoted in Romans 2:24 from the Greek, “the name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles because of you”.\f*
\v 6 Therefore my people shall know my name; therefore in that day \add they shall know\add* that I am he who speaks: here am I.”\f + \fr 52:6 \ft Heb.: \fqa ’ani hu\ft , “I am he”; compare 41:4; 43:10, 13, and the Greek “I am” in John 8:24, 58.\f*
\q1
\v 7 How beautiful upon the mountains
\q2 are the feet of him who brings good news,
\q1 who proclaims peace, who brings good news of good things,
\q2 who proclaims salvation,
\q2 who says to Zion, “Your God reigns!”\f + \fr 52:7 \ft Note: Quoted in Romans 10:15; see also Nahum 1:15.\f*
\q1
\v 8 The voice of your watchmen! They lift up their voice;
\q2 together they shout for joy,
\q1 for eye to eye they shall see
\q2 when the \nd LORD\nd* returns to Zion.\f + \fr 52:8 \ft Or: \fqa they see it with their own eyes\ft . Lit. \fqa eye in eye\ft .\f*\f + \fr 52:8 \ft Or: \fqa brings back Zion\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 9 \zpl Break forth\zpl*, \zpl shout for joy\zpl* together,
\q2 you waste places of Jerusalem,
\q1 for the \nd LORD\nd* has comforted his people;
\q2 he has redeemed Jerusalem.
\q1
\v 10 The \nd LORD\nd* has bared his holy arm
\q2 before the eyes of all the nations,
\q1 and all the ends of the earth shall see
\q2 the salvation of our God.
\q1
\v 11 \zpl Depart\zpl*, \zpl depart\zpl*, \zpl go out\zpl* from there;
\q2 \zpl touch\zpl* no unclean thing;
\q1 \zpl go out\zpl* from the midst of her; \zpl purify yourselves\zpl*,
\q2 you who bear the vessels of the \nd LORD\nd*.\f + \fr 52:11 \ft Note: Quoted in 2 Corinthians 6:17.\f*
\q1
\v 12 For \zpl you\zpl* shall not go out in haste,
\q2 and \zpl you\zpl* shall not go in flight,
\q1 for the \nd LORD\nd* will go before \zpl you\zpl*,
\q2 and the God of Israel will be \zpl your\zpl* rear guard.\f + \fr 52:12 \ft Lit.: \fqa the one gathering you up\ft , that is, bringing up the rear.\f*
\s1 The Suffering and Exaltation of the Servant
\q1
\v 13 Behold, my servant shall act wisely;
\q2 he shall be high and lifted up and greatly exalted.\f + \fr 52:13 \ft Or: \fqa shall prosper\ft ; or \fqa shall deal prudently \ft (KJV). The Hebrew word includes both acting wisely and succeeding.\f*
\q1
\v 14 Just as many were appalled at you—
\q2 his appearance was so marred, beyond that of a man,
\q2 and his form beyond that of the sons of men—\f + \fr 52:14 \ft Heb.: \fqa at you\ft ; the Syriac and the Targum read \fqa at him\ft . Lit. \fqa so marred from a man was his appearance\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 15 so shall he sprinkle many nations;
\q2 kings shall shut their mouths because of him;
\q1 for that which had not been told them they shall see,
\q2 and that which they had not heard they shall understand.\f + \fr 52:15 \ft Or: \fqa startle many nations\ft . The Hebrew verb elsewhere means “sprinkle”, as a priest sprinkles blood or water (Leviticus 4:6; 16:14); the Greek (LXX) has “many nations shall marvel at him”, and some scholars read the Hebrew as “startle”.\f*\f + \fr 52:15 \ft Note: Quoted in Romans 15:21.\f*
\c 53
\q1
\v 1 Who has believed our report?
\q2 And to whom has the arm of the \nd LORD\nd* been revealed?\f + \fr 53:1 \ft Or: \fqa what we have heard\ft . Quoted in John 12:38 and Romans 10:16.\f*
\q1
\v 2 He grew up before him like a tender shoot,
\q2 and like a root out of dry ground;
\q1 he had no form or majesty that we should look at him,
\q2 and no appearance that we should desire him.
\q1
\v 3 He was despised and rejected by men,
\q2 a man of sorrows and acquainted with grief;
\q1 and like one from whom men hide their faces
\q2 he was despised, and we did not esteem him.\f + \fr 53:3 \ft Heb.: \fqa a man of pains and knowing sickness\ft . The same two words are used in v. 4.\f*\f + \fr 53:3 \ft Or: \fqa and as one who hid his face from us\ft . Lit. \fqa like a hiding of face from him\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 4 Surely he has borne our griefs
\q2 and carried our sorrows;
\q1 yet we considered him stricken,
\q2 struck by God, and afflicted.\f + \fr 53:4 \ft Heb.: \fqa our sicknesses\ft  … \fqa our pains\ft ; Matthew 8:17 quotes this verse so.\f*
\q1
\v 5 But he was pierced for our transgressions;
\q2 he was crushed for our iniquities;
\q1 the chastisement of our peace was upon him,
\q2 and by his stripes we are healed.\f + \fr 53:5 \ft Or: \fqa wounded \ft (KJV). The Hebrew verb means to pierce through, as with a sword.\f*\f + \fr 53:5 \ft Lit.: \fqa the chastisement that brought our peace\ft .\f*\f + \fr 53:5 \ft Heb.: \fqa chaburah\ft , the welt left by a blow; KJV \fqa stripes\ft . Quoted in 1 Peter 2:24.\f*
\q1
\v 6 All we like sheep have gone astray;
\q2 we have turned, every one, to his own way;
\q1 and the \nd LORD\nd* has laid on him
\q2 the iniquity of us all.\f + \fr 53:6 \ft Lit.: \fqa has caused to fall on him\ft ; the same verb is translated “made intercession” in v. 12.\f*
\q1
\v 7 He was oppressed, and he was afflicted,
\q2 yet he did not open his mouth;
\q1 like a lamb that is led to the slaughter,
\q2 and like a sheep that before its shearers is silent,
\q2 so he did not open his mouth.\f + \fr 53:7 \ft Or: \fqa He was oppressed, yet he humbled himself and did not open his mouth\ft . Quoted in Acts 8:32–33 from the Greek.\f*
\q1
\v 8 By oppression and judgment he was taken away;
\q2 and as for his generation, who considered
\q1 that he was cut off out of the land of the living,
\q2 stricken for the transgression of my people?\f + \fr 53:8 \ft Or: \fqa He was taken from prison and from judgment \ft (KJV); or \fqa after arrest and sentence he was taken away\ft .\f*\f + \fr 53:8 \ft Or: \fqa who shall declare his generation? \ft (KJV); or \fqa who of his generation considered\ft .\f*\f + \fr 53:8 \ft Lit.: \fqa a stroke to them\ft ; the Hebrew ending can be read as singular, “to him”. A Dead Sea Scroll (1QIsaª) reads \fqa he was stricken\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 9 And they made his grave with the wicked,
\q2 and with a rich man in his death,
\q1 although he had done no violence,
\q2 and there was no deceit in his mouth.\f + \fr 53:9 \ft Or: \fqa his grave was assigned with the wicked\ft . The Hebrew verb is singular, “he gave”, with no subject stated.\f*\f + \fr 53:9 \ft Heb.: \fqa in his deaths\ft , a plural form; a Dead Sea Scroll (1QIsaª) reads \fqa his tomb\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 10 Yet it pleased the \nd LORD\nd* to crush him; he has put him to grief.
\q2 When his soul makes a guilt offering,
\q1 he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days,
\q2 and the pleasure of the \nd LORD\nd* shall prosper in his hand.\f + \fr 53:10 \ft Lit.: \fqa he made him sick\ft ; or, with the Dead Sea Scroll 1QIsaª, \fqa and he pierced him\ft .\f*\f + \fr 53:10 \ft Or: \fqa when you make his soul a guilt offering \ft (KJV \fqa an offering for sin\ft ). The verb can be read as “you” (addressed to God) or as “it” (his soul). Heb. \fqa ’asham\ft , the guilt offering of Leviticus 5:14–6:7.\f*
\q1
\v 11 Out of the anguish of his soul he shall see and be satisfied;
\q2 by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many,
\q2 and he shall bear their iniquities.\f + \fr 53:11 \ft Text: MT \fqa he shall see\ft ; two Dead Sea Scrolls (1QIsaª, 1QIsaᵇ) and the Greek (LXX) read \fqa he shall see light\ft .\f*\f + \fr 53:11 \ft Or: \fqa by the knowledge of him\ft ; or \fqa by his knowledge my servant, the righteous one, shall make many to be accounted righteous\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 12 Therefore I will divide him a portion with the many,
\q2 and he shall divide the spoil with the strong,
\q1 because he poured out his soul to death
\q2 and was numbered with the transgressors;
\q1 yet he bore the sin of many,
\q2 and made intercession for the transgressors.\f + \fr 53:12 \ft Or: \fqa I will give him the many as his portion, and he shall take the strong as spoil\ft .\f*\f + \fr 53:12 \ft Note: Quoted in Luke 22:37; Mark 15:28 (TR, Maj.).\f*
